          "type": "terms",
          "items": [
            [
              "Net change",
              "Integral of a rate, or final amount minus initial amount."
            ],
            [
              "Accumulation function",
              "A function defined by an integral with a variable bound."
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "md": {
            "file": "learn.md"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "formulas",
          "title": "Formulas to know",
          "items": [
            {
              "label": "Endpoint evaluation",
              "latex": "\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx=F(b)-F(a),\\quad F^\\prime=f",
              "note": "For continuous f on the interval."
            },
            {
              "label": "Variable bounds",
              "latex": "\\frac{d}{dx}\\int_{u(x)}^{v(x)}f(t)\\,dt=f(v(x))v^\\prime(x)-f(u(x))u^\\prime(x)",
              "note": "Use continuity at the bounds and the chain rule."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "warn",
          "title": "Common errors",
          "md": "Evaluate the antiderivative at the lower endpoint too. A moving lower bound contributes negatively. Include the derivative of each bound."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "exam",
          "title": "Written exam practice",
          "md": "Look at the requested operation first. An integral with fixed bounds asks for a value, and a derivative of an accumulation function usually calls for direct use of the theorem."
        }
      ]
    },
